# Chapter Sixteen — Tuesday

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.1. Coda. Henry tells Nell badly. Nell refuses the completion. The window is finished. The place card. Henry visits Ruth without being asked. The title is not decoded. No interaction branches.

## Scene 49 — Badly

= Present: Henry, Nell, Marian, Robert, at the Holts' house in Pasadena.

= Immediate pressure: Henry promised to come and tell her badly. He is an hour late and has missed a train doing it.

INT./EXT. THE HOLTS' BUNGALOW, PASADENA - MORNING

The Holts' house is a bungalow on a street of bungalows, with a porch and a lemon tree and a hose coiled wrong. At twenty past nine on Monday, Robert opens the door before Henry knocks. He has the newspaper under his arm and has not read it.

ROBERT
You're here.

HENRY
Yes.

ROBERT
She's in the yard. Marian's in the kitchen. I answer the door. I'm sorry, come in.

He does not move out of the doorway.

ROBERT
I took Lydia. Half past seven, Union Station. One suitcase; the other went ahead Friday. She stood on the step of the day coach and looked down the platform, both ways, until the conductor made her sit. She didn't ask me who she was looking for.

HENRY
What did you say?

ROBERT
I said, "He's not coming." She said, "I know. He's with her." I don't know how she knew.

Henry does not tell him.

ROBERT
She's on it. It went.

He steps back and lets Henry in.

The kitchen has a window onto the yard. Marian is at the sink with her sleeves pushed up, not washing anything. Through the glass, Nell is standing on the grass with her back to the house and a cup in her hand, looking at nothing in particular, the way people stand in yards.

MARIAN
She went out there when the telephone rang at eight and it was nobody. She said she'd come in when you came.

HENRY
I'll go out.

MARIAN
No. She said she'd come in.

Marian taps on the glass once with her ring. Nell turns, sees Henry, and comes in without hurrying, and puts the cup on the drainboard, and sits at the kitchen table, and puts her hands on it.

NELL
Say it.

Robert looks at Marian. Marian does not leave, and does not sit.

HENRY
Ruth is at headquarters. Ward arrested her at ten to eight in my office for Paul's death. I was there. She said it in his hearing.

NELL
Say what she said.

HENRY
Paul went up the stair with the packet. She went up after him. He held out her cover, with his pencil on it, and asked her to help him say it so that you could stand to hear it. She said no. She took hold of the loose sheets. It tore. She put both hands on him and pushed. He caught the lip. She went down on her knees and held his wrist. He said, "Ruth," and then, "Help me." The button was one pace from her. She saw it. The pages were between her and it. She let go with one hand to gather them. He said "help me" again. She said no. She let go with the other.

Nell's hands do not move on the table.

NELL
Not my name.

HENRY
No. Hers. Then "help me."

NELL
He'd stopped using mine.

HENRY
Yes.

NELL
Good.

She says the word the way Ruth said it in the corridor, and hears that, and does not take it back.

NELL
Why?

HENRY
She said, "Not with my name on them." She said it was that small. He was going to read it all to you that night in his own words, with her in it, and do it well.

NELL
He would have.

HENRY
Yes.

NELL
Badly. He would have done it badly, and it would have been his.

She looks at the table.

NELL
What else.

HENRY
She asked me to tell you the sentence was hers. The one he said when he left. All of it. She said you know, and she wanted it said again in a room with a policeman in it.

NELL
It's said.

HENRY
Yes.

NELL
Then it's said.

Marian turns the tap on, and off.

MARIAN
Did she ask about the funeral?

HENRY
She arranged it. Thursday at eleven. She won't be there.

MARIAN
No.

ROBERT
We'll go.

MARIAN
Yes, Robert.

He nods. He goes out to the porch and puts the newspaper down on the chair there, and comes back without it, and stands in the doorway.

Nell stands.

NELL
You missed the train.

HENRY
Yes.

NELL
I paid for the attempt.

HENRY
I know what you paid for.

NELL
I'm not asking for it back.

She goes to the drainboard and picks up the cup she left there, and looks at it, and puts it in the sink.

NELL
Havel is at the bungalow tomorrow at ten. I'm going to tell her. I want you to drive.

HENRY
As what?

NELL
As the one person there who has heard all of it.

Henry says yes.

## Scene 50 — Permission

= Present: Nell, Havel, Henry, at Bungalow Six.

= Immediate pressure: the bedroom is scheduled, the stage is standing, and the rights holder has come to say a word Havel cannot put on a schedule.

INT./EXT. TREMONT ANNEX, BUNGALOW SIX - MORNING

Tuesday at ten the gate at Tremont Annex is open and the production report is gone from its clip. Havel meets them outside the bungalow with the gray file under her arm. She has not slept either. She offers Nell her hand first, and Nell takes it.

Inside, the table, the four folding chairs. Havel puts the file down and does not open it.

HAVEL
Ward telephoned last night.

NELL
Then you know.

HAVEL
I know she's charged. He didn't say with what she said.

NELL
No. He wouldn't.

Havel waits, and Nell does not give it to her, and Havel accepts that as the first thing she is not going to get today.

HAVEL
I am going to say something once, so that it's said, and then I'll listen. I want that scene. I've wanted it since the first daily. I've been in this business twenty-two years and I have wanted two things in it the way I want that room, and the other one I got, and it was not as good as this. I'm telling you so that you know what you're saying no to.

NELL
I heard it on Friday. I know what I'm saying no to.

HAVEL
All right.

NELL
The originals come to me when Ward is finished with them. The folder and the six reels. The workprint and the transfers, and the photostats, and the nine shooting copies, and whatever you exposed before the hold: sealed. Not destroyed. Marian wanted it left with her. I'm leaving it with her. Attributed to June Avery on every can and every sleeve. Nobody plays it.

HAVEL
For how long?

NELL
I don't know.

HAVEL
That's what you said on Friday.

NELL
It's still true.

Havel takes it.

HAVEL
And the passes.

NELL
Ruth's pages are Ruth's. She can't give permission from where she is. I'm not going to ask her. Neither are you.

HAVEL
They're the best thing in the picture.

NELL
Yes.

HAVEL
You said so yourself, in that room. "The new ending is better."

NELL
I said it. It's true. It doesn't buy anything.

Havel looks at the closed file.

HAVEL
Rusk loses the picture.

NELL
He lost one before. He seems to have come through it better than she did.

Havel does not answer that. Henry, at the edge of the room, watches her not answer it, and thinks it is the most professional thing he has seen her do.

HAVEL
The bedroom is standing. Monday's day is still on the board.

NELL
Then shoot something else in it. It's a room. Nobody owns a room.

HAVEL
What?

NELL
Have her answer first. Before she closes the wardrobe. Have the man come in. Let him. It'll feel false.

HAVEL
Yes, it will.

NELL
Then it's yours.

Havel takes that in, and it goes somewhere, and Henry sees it arrive.

HAVEL
I'll write it up. The same inventory to Ward.

NELL
No changes between them.

HAVEL
No changes.

She opens the file at last, to a blank memorandum, and writes the date. She stops.

HAVEL
Miss Avery. When you said "permission" on Friday, and I kept writing "clearance." I knew the difference. I wanted it to be the one I could get.

NELL
I know.

She stands.

NELL
Say permission when you write it.

Havel writes it.

## Scene 51 — The window

= Present: Henry, Edwin, Bell, in Tolland's motor court.

= Immediate pressure: none. It is a week later, and it is closing time, and Henry has walked past on purpose.

EXT. TOLLAND'S MOTOR COURT - EVENING

A week to the day after the supper, at six on Wednesday, the motor court is open to customers for another ten minutes. Cars come out from under the canopy with their lights on. The gray screen is gone.

The window beside the service door is finished. Six mannequins sit at a table laid for six under a lamp, with the meal painted on the plates. Where the moon hung, the scenery has been painted the color of the rest of the wall. There is no seventh chair. There is no rail.

Henry stands where Bell stood.

The service door opens and Edwin comes out in his working suit, with his keys.

EDWIN
Mr. Calder.

HENRY
The chief wanted seven.

EDWIN
He did. I said six. He asked why. I told him seven was an accusation.

HENRY
And eight?

EDWIN
There is no room for eight. I measured.

They look at the window.

EDWIN
The stair is still there. They bolted the bay. It is a rail now like the others.

HENRY
Good.

EDWIN
I do not go up.

He takes something from his inside pocket. It is a place card, folded once, a little soft at the fold from a week in a jacket.

EDWIN
Miss Shaw left this with me on Monday at a quarter to seven, on her way to the train. Mr. Holt waited in the court with the engine running. She said you would come for your coat.

HENRY
I didn't leave a coat.

EDWIN
She knew that.

Henry takes the card. He does not open it.

EDWIN
She came in through the court. She wanted to see the window before they finished it. Mr. Bell let her in. He is not permitted to; he did.

Across the court, Bell is in his booth with his cap on. He looks at Henry and lifts one hand from the counter and puts it down again, which is what he has.

HENRY
Did she say anything?

EDWIN
She said, "Six is right." Then she said, "Tell him I would have put him beside me." I have told you.

HENRY
Thank you.

EDWIN
She said it twice. The second time she said "beside her." I do not know which one she meant, and she did not, and I am not going to choose for her.

He goes back to the service door.

EDWIN
Mr. Calder. The Winter Room seats a hundred and forty at lunch. There is no need to know anyone.

HENRY
I'll come.

EDWIN
Thursday. There is sole.

The door closes behind him. Bell comes out of his booth without being asked and unlocks the motor-court door for Henry, though it is not yet closing time and the door is open, and holds it, and Henry goes through it.

On Hill Street he opens the card. HENRY CALDER, in green ink, in a hand that did not hurry. On the back, a faint number, crossed out twice. Beneath the crossings, nothing.

He puts it in the pocket where the pocketknife is, and walks.

## Scene 52 — Tuesday

= Present: Henry, Ruth, a matron.

= Immediate pressure: nobody sent for him.

INT. COUNTY JAIL, VISITING ROOM - AFTERNOON

The visiting room at the county jail is a long table with a low wooden barrier down its middle and chairs on both sides. A matron sits at the end with a clock. The wood of the table has been wiped so many times that the grain stands up out of it.

Henry sits on the outside. He has been there four minutes when the inner door opens and Ruth is brought through it, in gray, without her coat.

She stops when she sees who it is. Then she comes and sits.

RUTH
I didn't send for you.

HENRY
No.

RUTH
Who invited you?

HENRY
Nobody.

RUTH
Who invited you to invite yourself?

HENRY
The card says visiting is Tuesdays.

RUTH
The card.

HENRY
There's a card.

She almost laughs. It does not arrive, and she lets it not arrive.

RUTH
Did they print it?

HENRY
All eleven words.

RUTH
Who came?

HENRY
Nell. Marian and Robert. Edwin, in the suit. Mrs. Vale. Bell, with his cap off. Havel sent nothing, which was correct. A card came for Nell from Portland the day after. Green ink. She didn't say what was in it.

RUTH
Nell.

HENRY
She said no to Havel. Once. The originals come to her. The rest is sealed with Marian, under June's name. Nobody plays it.

RUTH
And mine.

HENRY
Yours are yours. She won't ask you. Havel won't.

Ruth looks at the table between them.

RUTH
They were good.

HENRY
They were.

RUTH
That's not a defense.

HENRY
No. It's a fact. Nell said it too. She said it doesn't buy anything.

RUTH
She's right.

She puts her hands on the table, on her side of the barrier, flat, the way Nell puts hers.

RUTH
My lawyer wants me to say it was the wrist. The version I gave you Sunday. He says the room in your office was three people and no notebook and the detective's memory, and juries like a woman on her knees.

HENRY
What did you tell him?

RUTH
I told him I said it in that room so it would be the shape it is. He said that was not a legal position. I said no, it was a sentence, and I wrote it, and I'm keeping it.

HENRY
Ward will say it the way it was said.

RUTH
I know. That's why I said it to him.

The matron looks at her clock and does not say anything yet.

RUTH
I don't want to be forgiven, Henry. Lydia tried it at a distance of six feet and couldn't manage it. Don't try it through a fence.

HENRY
I haven't got it to give.

RUTH
Then what have you got?

HENRY
Tuesday.

She looks at him.

RUTH
Is this a job?

HENRY
No.

RUTH
You came to my office.

HENRY
You never gave me your home address.

RUTH
I have one now.

HENRY
I found it.

That one arrives. She does not laugh; her face does something older than laughing and puts it away again.

The matron stands.

Ruth stands with her. She takes her hands off the table, and looks at the place where they were, and then at him.

RUTH
Tuesday.

HENRY
Tuesday.

She goes to the inner door. She does not look back, and he does not expect her to; she did it once, on the stairs, and once was what it was for.

The door closes. Henry sits for a moment longer at the wiped table, and then he goes out into the street, where it is five thirty-seven on a Tuesday, and the light is going, and he has an office to open.
